<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Falerii</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.) · capital Falerii</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chief city of the Faliscans, a people who spoke a language close to Latin but lived within Etruscan culture, destroyed by Rome in 241 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Falerii, later called Falerii Veteres, stood on a steep tufa plateau at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civita-castellana">Civita Castellana</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ager-faliscus">Ager Faliscus</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cimino">Monte Cimino</a>. It developed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlements of the 9th and 8th centuries BCE. The Faliscans spoke Faliscan, an Italic language closely related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, though their art, cults and politics were tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Falerii was famous for the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juno-curitis">Juno Curitis</a>, whose festival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> described, and for its temples decorated with fine terracottas. Its workshops produced Faliscan red-figure vases in the 4th century BCE. The city usually allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veii">Veii</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; Roman legend tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-furius-camillus">Camillus</a>, besieging it in 394 BCE, sent back a schoolmaster who tried to hand over his pupils, and the Faliscans surrendered in admiration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After further wars Falerii revolted in 241 BCE, just after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, and was crushed within days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroyed the hilltop city and moved its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falerii-novi">Falerii Novi</a> on open ground about 6 km away, whose walls still stand. The old site was resettled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civita-castellana">Civita Castellana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/falerii.jpg" alt="Falerii" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Croberto68 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Maria_di_Falleri_10.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Falerii</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Faliscan polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Faliscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Faliscans, Falerii Veteres</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-520&amp;focus=falerii" aria-label="Falerii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4980 673 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Falerii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5300 929L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-520&amp;focus=falerii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

